# Java Shallow Copy vs Deep Copy

When working with objects in Java, copying an object can be confusing.

Consider this question:

> If we copy an object and later modify the copy, will the original object also change?

The answer depends on **how the object was copied**.

This README explains:

* Reference Copy
* Shallow Copy
* Deep Copy
* The difference between them
* Real examples using nested objects

---

# 1. Reference Copy

Let's start with the simplest type of copying.

```java
Student s1 = new Student("Nikitha");

Student s2 = s1;
```

This does **not create a new object**.

Both `s1` and `s2` refer to the same object.

```text
s1 ────────┐
           ▼
       Student Object
           ▲
s2 ────────┘
```

Example:

```java
class Student {

    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student s1 = new Student("Nikitha");

        Student s2 = s1;

        s2.name = "Rahul";

        System.out.println(s1.name);
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Rahul
```

Why?

Because `s1` and `s2` point to the **same object**.

---

# 2. What is a Shallow Copy?

A **shallow copy** creates a new outer object.

However, if the object contains references to other objects, those references are copied.

This means the original object and copied object can still share nested objects.

Consider:

```java
class Address {

    String city;

    Address(String city) {
        this.city = city;
    }
}

class Student {

    String name;
    Address address;

    Student(String name, Address address) {
        this.name = name;
        this.address = address;
    }
}
```

A `Student` contains:

```text
Student
   │
   ├── name
   │
   └── Address
```

---

# 3. Shallow Copy Example

```java
class Address {

    String city;

    Address(String city) {
        this.city = city;
    }
}

class Student {

    String name;
    Address address;

    Student(String name, Address address) {
        this.name = name;
        this.address = address;
    }

    Student(Student other) {

        this.name = other.name;

        // Same Address object is shared
        this.address = other.address;
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Address address =
                new Address("Bengaluru");

        Student s1 =
                new Student("Nikitha", address);

        Student s2 =
                new Student(s1);

        s2.address.city = "Mysuru";

        System.out.println(s1.address.city);
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Mysuru
```

Even though we created a new `Student` object, both students still refer to the same `Address`.

---

## Visual Representation

```text
Original Student (s1)
        │
        ▼
   Address Object
   city = Bengaluru
        ▲
        │
Copied Student (s2)
```

After:

```java
s2.address.city = "Mysuru";
```

The shared object changes.

Therefore:

```text
s1.address.city → Mysuru
s2.address.city → Mysuru
```

This is a **Shallow Copy**.

---

# 4. What is a Deep Copy?

A **deep copy** creates completely independent objects.

Not only is the outer object copied, but its nested objects are also copied.

```text
Original Student
       │
       ▼
   Address Object
       │
       ▼
   Bengaluru


Copied Student
       │
       ▼
   New Address Object
       │
       ▼
   Bengaluru
```

Now the two students do not share the same `Address`.

---

# 5. Deep Copy Example

```java
class Address {

    String city;

    Address(String city) {
        this.city = city;
    }

    Address(Address other) {
        this.city = other.city;
    }
}

class Student {

    String name;
    Address address;

    Student(String name, Address address) {
        this.name = name;
        this.address = address;
    }

    Student(Student other) {

        this.name = other.name;

        // Create a NEW Address object
        this.address =
                new Address(other.address);
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Address address =
                new Address("Bengaluru");

        Student s1 =
                new Student("Nikitha", address);

        Student s2 =
                new Student(s1);

        s2.address.city = "Mysuru";

        System.out.println(s1.address.city);
        System.out.println(s2.address.city);
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Bengaluru
Mysuru
```

Now the objects are completely independent.

---

# 6. Visual Comparison

## Shallow Copy

```text
s1                    s2
 │                     │
 ▼                     ▼
Student              Student
   │                    │
   └───────┐    ┌────────┘
           ▼
        Address
```

Both objects share the same nested `Address`.

---

## Deep Copy

```text
s1                    s2
 │                     │
 ▼                     ▼
Student              Student
 │                     │
 ▼                     ▼
Address              Address
```

Each object has its own independent nested object.

---

# 7. Shallow Copy vs Deep Copy

| Shallow Copy                    | Deep Copy                              |
| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Creates a new outer object      | Creates completely independent objects |
| Nested references may be shared | Nested objects are also copied         |
| Changes can affect both objects | Changes remain independent             |
| Faster and uses less memory     | Can use more memory                    |
| Simple to create                | More complex                           |

---

# 8. Where Does `clone()` Come In?

Java provides a `clone()` mechanism through the `Cloneable` interface.

Example:

```java
class Student implements Cloneable {

    String name;

    @Override
    protected Object clone()
            throws CloneNotSupportedException {

        return super.clone();
    }
}
```

`super.clone()` typically performs a **shallow copy**.

This means reference fields inside the object can still point to the same nested objects.

For a deep copy, you must explicitly copy nested mutable objects as well.

---

# 9. A Common Real-World Example

Imagine a user profile:

```text
User
 │
 ├── Name
 │
 ├── Email
 │
 └── Address
```

If you create a copy of the user:

```text
Original User
        ↓
     Address

Copied User
        ↓
     Address
```

If both users share the same address object and one copy changes the city, the other copy may also see that change.

This can create unexpected bugs.

A deep copy avoids this problem by creating separate nested objects.

---

# 🧠 Easy Way to Remember

```text
Reference Copy
→ Same object

Shallow Copy
→ New outer object
→ Shared nested objects

Deep Copy
→ New outer object
→ New nested objects
```

---

# 🔑 Final Takeaway

The main difference is not simply whether a **new object** is created.

The important question is:

> **Are the nested objects also copied?**

### Shallow Copy

```text
New Outer Object
+
Shared Nested Objects
```

### Deep Copy

```text
New Outer Object
+
New Nested Objects
```

Understanding this concept helps you avoid unexpected changes when working with:

* Nested objects
* Object references
* Collections
* Cloning
* Data transfer objects
* Real-world Java applications

---

⭐ **Key Rule:**

> If two objects share a mutable nested object, changing that nested object can affect both. Deep copying prevents this by creating independent nested objects.
